From: nixos-m4pro
Subject: [PATCH] nvme: apple: T6040: wake a quiesced ANS, hand it on quiesced

The nixos-m4pro boot chain hands ANS over quiesced (m1n1 patch 0005); U-Boot
adopted it without a handshake and waited forever for CSTS.RDY. Run the INIT
handshake whenever the controller is disabled and quiesce ANS again at
hand-off, which Linux nvme-apple (kernel patch 0021) wakes.
---
--- a/drivers/nvme/nvme_apple.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/nvme_apple.c
@@ -651,8 +651,14 @@
 		return !ofnode_has_property(ofnode_path("/chosen"),
 					    "asahi,nvme-adopt");
 
-	return !nvme_readq(priv->nvmmu + ANS_NVMMU_BASE_ASQ) &&
-	       !readl(priv->base + ANS_MAX_PEND_CMDS_CTRL);
+	/*
+	 * J614s chain (nixos-m4pro): every m1n1 stage that used NVMe hands
+	 * ANS over quiesced (AP and IOP power 0x10, CPU running), so the
+	 * controller reads disabled but still programmed, and only a new
+	 * INIT handshake brings it back.  Adopting it instead waits forever
+	 * for CSTS.RDY.
+	 */
+	return true;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -898,6 +904,23 @@
 	struct apple_nvme_priv *priv = dev_get_priv(dev);
 	u32 ctrl;
 
+	if (priv->hw->post_m4 && priv->woken && !priv->hw->keep_cc_fields) {
+		/*
+		 * J614s chain (nixos-m4pro): hand ANS on the way it came,
+		 * quiesced with its CPU running, as m1n1 does.  Linux's
+		 * nvme-apple wakes it with INIT and the firmware asks for new
+		 * buffers; ours stay reserved (apple_nvme_fdt_fixup()), as the
+		 * firmware may still write them.  Sleep would crash it.
+		 */
+		if (nvme_retire_io_queues(dev))
+			printf("%s: could not delete the I/O queues\n", dev->name);
+		if (apple_nvme_disable_post_m4(priv))
+			printf("%s: controller did not disable\n", dev->name);
+		if (apple_rtkit_shutdown(priv->rtk, APPLE_RTKIT_PWR_STATE_QUIESCED))
+			printf("%s: could not quiesce the ANS\n", dev->name);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	if (priv->hw->post_m4) {
 		/*
 		 * Hand the session over with RTKit live and the controller
